       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 4250 mg three times daily comes to 12,750 mg/day, which is about 5 times the usual maximum for metformin. The FDA-labeled maximum is 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release, given as 850 mg three times daily. A single 4,250 mg dose is also far above the usual maximum single dose of about 1,000 mg. A dose this high carries a serious risk of lactic acidosis, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nThis may be a typo. Possible intended doses include:\n- **850 mg PO TID with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the max labeled dose)\n- **425 mg PO TID** (less common, since it's not a standard tablet strength)\n- **500 mg or 1,000 mg PO BID or TID**, depending on the titration plan\n\nPlease tell me the dose you intended. If 4250 mg is deliberate, tell me that and I'll enter it with a note documenting the rationale. I'd still recommend checking it with pharmacy first."
      }
